## Layout

```
src/{{ package_name }}/
├── config.py       # pydantic-settings; paths & names via {{ package_name | upper }}__* env vars
├── schemas.py      # explicit Spark schemas (+ pydantic mirrors) for every dataset
├── transforms.py   # pure batch DataFrame -> DataFrame functions
├── streaming.py    # Kafka parsing, event-time windows, watermark/dedup logic
└── jobs/           # entrypoints that own I/O and compose transforms
tests/              # session-scoped local SparkSession fixture in conftest.py
```

## The architecture rule that matters

**All logic in `transforms.py`, all I/O in `jobs/`.** Transforms take
DataFrames and return DataFrames — no reads, no writes, no SparkSession
argument. That makes every piece of business logic testable in milliseconds
with `spark.createDataFrame`, and jobs become trivial glue that rarely breaks.

Schema inference is banned for production reads; every dataset has an explicit
schema in `schemas.py`.

`deduplicate_events` intentionally uses `row_number` over a partition window.
`orderBy(...).dropDuplicates(...)` cannot guarantee which row survives the
deduplication shuffle. Replace the example value tie-breaker with the source's
sequence number or ingestion timestamp when one exists.

The streaming example shows the concepts interviewers usually probe: Kafka
offsets, event time, watermarks, bounded deduplication state, durable
checkpoints, and append-safe file output. The cluster or `spark-submit` command
must supply `spark-sql-kafka-0-10` matching its exact Spark and Scala versions;
the Python package alone cannot safely choose that deployment artifact.

